## What this is

The scanner flagged `res.sendFile(path.resolve(...))` on line 14 of `privacyPolicyProof.ts` as a potential **CWE-22 Path Traversal** because `path.resolve()` can incorporate user-controlled input into a file path. In this specific case the path is a **hardcoded string literal**, so this is almost certainly a **false positive**. The rule fires on any `sendFile` call wrapped in `path.resolve()` regardless of whether the argument is dynamic.

## Why it matters

If the path *were* derived from user input (e.g., `req.params.file`), an attacker could supply `../../etc/passwd` and read arbitrary files on the server. Even though this instance is static, leaving the pattern unaddressed trains reviewers to ignore the warning and makes future edits risky.

## How to fix it

Since the path is already a constant, the simplest fix is to **remove `path.resolve()`** and pass the literal string directly, or wrap it in a small helper that validates the resolved path stays inside the intended directory. This silences the scanner and hardens the code against accidental future edits.

```ts
// Before (line 14)
res.sendFile(path.resolve('frontend/dist/frontend/assets/private/thank-you.jpg'))

// After – explicit, scanner-friendly, and safe
const SAFE_DIR = path.resolve('frontend/dist/frontend/assets/private')
const filePath = path.resolve(SAFE_DIR, 'thank-you.jpg')
if (!filePath.startsWith(SAFE_DIR)) {
  return res.status(403).send('Forbidden')
}
res.sendFile(filePath)
```

